Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 105, "THE TRAITOR'S MEMORY"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 15 script pages of a 50-minute episode. Canon adjustments, made so it holds with the approved film: (1) the Season 1 bible says Torin joined the Wardens at 22, but the film makes him 42 and a Warden who "fell out of the old order twenty years ago", so he cannot have joined at 22: here Malachar places him with the Wardens at 18 and the council expels him at 22, and Perrin Hask alone keeps faith with him; (2) the bible has the Lamp whisper to Torin, but Continuity Lock 7 says the Lamp speaks to no one but Seraphine: here the Lamp speaks only to Seraphine, and hears Torin through the Warden key he takes; (3) Auren's vision shows Hask's room and never Torin's face (Lock 6); (4) Torin does not tell Malachar the boy's name, which is why the film still needs "the Warden confirmed it". Perrin Hask is a proposal. Torin's promise to Hask is the root of "you crossed the bridge for me" in the film. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. A WARDEN HOSPICE — A STONE HOUSE IN THE HIGH VALLEY — DAWN

A narrow iron bed under a small window. Beyond the glass, a white mountain going pink. An oil lamp turned low. A basin, a jug, a bunch of dried thyme tied to the bedpost.

PERRIN HASK, eighty-one, lies propped on three pillows. He was a big man once and the frame is still there, with nothing left on it. His hands lie on the blanket like tools put down.

The water in the jug trembles in a perfect ring. A low note rises under the floor, steady, dry, patient. Hask turns his head toward it, unafraid, the way a man turns to a neighbour at the door.

HASK
(a whisper, pleased)
There you are. You took your time.

The note holds. The door opens behind him and snow comes in on the shoulders of a grey coat. TORIN DRAVEN, forty-one, courteous, still, a soldier's stillness and a scholar's eyes. This is the first time we see his face. He stops in the doorway, hearing nothing. He sees only the old man smiling at the ceiling.

HASK (CONT'D)
You're late.

TORIN
The pass was shut.

HASK
It's always shut. You came anyway.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE WARDEN HOSPICE — HASK'S ROOM — MORNING

Torin sits by the bed with a bowl of broth he is not allowed to hold for long. Hask spoons it himself, badly, refusing help.

HASK
Say the catechism.

TORIN
Perrin.

HASK
Humour a dying man. What do the Wardens guard?

TORIN
(a small, weary smile; he has said it a thousand times)
The door. And whoever is on the far side of it.

HASK
And what do we ask of them?

TORIN
Nothing. We are not owed.

HASK
(satisfied)
There were four hundred of us when I took the oath. There are eleven now, and nine of those can't climb a stair. The council sold six of the seven keys for the price of a roof. I am the last one who still means it.

He lifts a hand toward his throat. Under the nightshirt, on a worn cord, hangs a small black iron key, no longer than a finger, its bow rubbed bright by eighty years of a thumb.

HASK (CONT'D)
You know what it opens.

TORIN
The second door of the Citadel.

HASK
The one the Lamp cannot refuse. A Warden with this can stand in the records and read, and the Lamp has to let him, whatever the Keeper says. The old masters made seven in case the Keeper ever failed.

TORIN
(carefully)
And has she?

HASK
(a shrug that costs him)
I have never seen a Keeper. I have only ever seen a door.

He lets the key fall back. He looks at Torin for a long time, with the patience of a man who has run out of other things to do.

HASK (CONT'D)
There is a boy.

Torin does not move at all.

HASK (CONT'D)
Isolde Vale had a son. Nine years old when she went. I kept a Warden's eye on the house for a year and then lost him: the city swallowed him. He will be twenty-three. He is the line, Torin. When I'm gone the key should go to him, with whatever I can still tell you about what it opens.

TORIN
(very evenly)
Why not wait for him to find you?

HASK
Because I am eighty-one and there's a draught in the lungs that wasn't there on Tuesday.

Torin takes the empty bowl and sets it on the sill. He is a man who has been taught to hide everything and has just discovered that he cannot hide this. He keeps his back to the bed.

TORIN
(quietly)
What was his name?

HASK
Vale. Auren.

TORIN
(not turning)
I'll find him.

INT. AN ORPHANAGE IN A RIVER TOWN — A DORMITORY — NIGHT (FLASHBACK, ABOUT THIRTY YEARS AGO)

Rain on a tin roof. A row of iron cots. On the last one a boy of twelve, THE BOY, lies wide awake with his eyes on the ceiling, a flood-water tidemark still on the wall of the room where they let him keep his father's coat.

The door opens. A MAN in a plain grey coat comes in without a lamp, forty by the look of him, with kind hands. He sits on the end of the cot, so gently the springs hardly note it. This is MALACHAR. We do not hear what the boy hears: the low note, faint, at the far edge of the rain.

MALACHAR
(softly)
You're awake.

THE BOY
(flat)
I'm always awake. It's the rain.

MALACHAR
Yes. It was the rain for me too, once.

He takes a square of paper from his pocket and begins to fold it, unhurried. The boy watches the hands.

THE BOY
Are you the man who's looking for somebody?

MALACHAR
(a small smile)
I'm the man who found him. You do not have to be anybody's yet, you know. Not mine. Not the orphanage's. Not even your own, for a while.

He finishes the bird. It sits on his palm, small and exact, one wing a little crooked. He sets it on the blanket beside the boy's hand.

THE BOY
(after a long time)
Will you come back?

MALACHAR
Every winter. Until you're ready to ask.

DISSOLVE TO:

EXT. A COUNTRY ROAD — DAY (FLASHBACK, THREE YEARS LATER)

A boy of fifteen with a suitcase and a father's coat too big at the shoulders climbs into the passenger seat of a grey car. The man at the wheel does not say anything. He only turns the heater up.

ACT TWO

INT. A STONE REFECTORY — THE WARDENS' HOUSE — DAY (FLASHBACK, THREE YEARS LATER)

Long tables, a cold hearth, twelve old men eating in a hall built for two hundred. At the head table, a younger Hask, fifty-eight, enormous, ruddy, scraping a pot. Before him stands a young man of eighteen in a good coat: Torin, trim, courteous, ready.

HASK
(not looking up)
A boy who reads Old Aurelian at eighteen is a gift or a spy. Which are you?

TORIN
Neither, sir.

HASK
(looks up; grins)
Good. A spy would have said "a gift." You can start with the kettle.

A rack of old brass puzzle-locks hangs behind him, each with two keyholes set a body's width apart.

HASK (CONT'D)
First thing a Warden learns. No door in the world opens for one pair of hands. Two keys, two people, or it isn't a door, it's a wall. Come here. Put your thumb there.

He puts Torin's right thumb in one hollow and his own in the other. The lock turns. Torin's face, for the first time in the episode, is simply a young man's, delighted.

DISSOLVE TO:

INT. THE WARDENS' COUNCIL ROOM — NIGHT (FLASHBACK, FOUR YEARS LATER)

Three old Wardens at a long table. A sheaf of pages on it, copied in a neat hand: an index of the Citadel's records, taken from a place it should never have left. Torin, twenty-two, stands before them with his hands behind his back, white and entirely calm.

COUNCILLOR
Who asked you to copy it?

TORIN
Nobody.

COUNCILLOR
Then who did you give it to?

TORIN
Nobody.

COUNCILLOR
(softly)
Then why does it have a rail ticket pressed in the pages?

Torin says nothing. At the foot of the table Hask has stood up, filling the room.

HASK
He meant no harm, and I'll answer for it.

COUNCILLOR
You will not. He is expelled from the order at the rising of the council, and the oath he was to swear on Sunday goes unsworn.

Torin turns, very slowly, and looks at Hask. There is a plea in his face he would never put in words. Hask understands it, or thinks he does.

INT. THE WARDENS' HOUSE — THE FRONT DOOR — NIGHT (FLASHBACK)

Torin on the step with a suitcase in a thin rain. Hask comes out behind him in his shirtsleeves and puts his own heavy winter coat round the young man's shoulders.

HASK
My door is not the council's door.

TORIN
(a whisper)
You don't know what I did.

HASK
No. And I have decided I can live without knowing.

He goes in. Torin stands in the rain in the coat. We hold on his face. He is not crying, because he was taught not to.

ACT THREE

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — LAB — DAY

Auren at his bench with a coin under the loupe. Nell is doing the crossword two benches away and humming the wrong tune. The coin gives its quiet note.

AUREN
(very softly)
Not here.

It is already too late. The lab dims at the edges. Nell looks up, alarmed, and finds that nobody is looking back.

INT. THE WARDEN HOSPICE — HASK'S ROOM — NIGHT (AUREN'S VISION)

Hush, flat gold, an oil lamp turned low. Auren stands in the doorway, uninvited, in his museum lanyard. In the iron bed an old man lies asleep with a key on a cord at his throat. At the window, with his back to us, a man in a grey coat. We see a shoulder, a hand, nothing else. His face is never turned toward the room.

The man crosses to the bed. He stops. He takes the cord in his fingers, very lightly, and lifts it over the sleeping head.

The old man's eyes open.

HASK
(not moving)
I wondered which of us would be the one.

The man at the bed does not speak. The key hangs from his fist.

HASK (CONT'D)
You have a face you make when you're about to do the thing. You've had it since you were eighteen. I used to hope I'd see it once and be wrong.

THE MAN
(hardly a voice)
Perrin. I'm sorry.

HASK
(a breath of laughter)
Yes. Take it. I'd rather it were in your hand than in the ground.

A silence. The note under the floor rises and holds.

HASK (CONT'D)
Promise me one thing.

THE MAN
Anything I can keep.

HASK
There's a boy. Whatever you're going to do with that, don't let it be used on him.

A very long beat. The man's shoulders do something small. Then, quite clearly:

THE MAN
I promise.

HASK
(shutting his eyes)
That's the first thing you've said tonight I believe.

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — LAB — DAY

Auren comes back like a swimmer breaking surface. He is on the floor between the benches with his head on Nell's folded cardigan, and Nell on her knees beside him with the stopwatch in one hand and a plain terror in her face.

NELL
Four minutes. Don't you dare say you're fine.

AUREN
(hoarse)
An old man forgave somebody.

NELL
What?

AUREN
For taking something. He let him. He said, "Take it." It was so... it was before. A very long time ago. Ithren's.

NELL
(carefully)
How do you know whose it was?

AUREN
(slowly; he does not)
Because who else would forgive that?

EXT. THE WARDEN HOSPICE — DAWN

The mountain. Snow. Torin stands alone on the step of the stone house in the grey coat, one hand shut tight in the pocket of it. Behind him the lamp in the window has gone out.

In his other hand is a paper bird, soft with thirty years of pockets, one wing crooked. He looks at it for a long time. Then he goes back inside.

INT. THE WARDEN HOSPICE — HASK'S ROOM — DAWN

Hask lies as he was, eyes closed, the basin and the thyme and the lamp gone cold. The window has turned the colour of milk. His hands are folded on the blanket.

Torin lays the bird on his chest, under the hands, and straightens the blanket. He sits down on the chair. He is holding the iron key, and his voice is a thing he does not recognise.

TORIN
(to the room)
I am the traitor.

It is the first time he has said it aloud. The note rises under the floor, once, steady, and holds.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE GUARDIAN CITADEL — THE RECORDS ROOM — NIGHT

The round chamber of pale stone, the alcoves with their grey tablets. Seraphine stands alone in the middle of the floor, with her cardigan buttoned wrong, as if she had been summoned before she finished dressing.

The blue lamps have all brightened at once.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
A key has changed hands that was not given.

SERAPHINE
(very still)
Whose?

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Perrin Hask's. He is gone. It was taken before his breath, and forgiven. I hear it as I hear the keys: the one who holds it has said a word.

SERAPHINE
What word?

THE LAMP (V.O.)
Traitor.

She closes her eyes. The lamps burn steadily on her face.

SERAPHINE
(low)
Then there is a Warden with the second door. And he knows what he is.

THE LAMP (V.O.)
(precisely; a thing near tenderness)
That is not the same as safe, Keeper.

SERAPHINE
No. I have been wrong about the kind ones before.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 105 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
